<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ʻai kapu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kapu was the ancient Hawaiian code of conduct, a system of laws and prohibitions reaching into daily life, gender roles, politics and religion; the word, related to the Polynesian tapu that gave English 'taboo', means forbidden but also sacred, and its opposite is noa. Breaking a kapu, even by accident, often meant immediate death. Commoners could not touch a chief's hair or nail clippings, look directly at him or hold their heads higher than his, and under the ʻai kapu men and women could not eat together and women were barred from pork, most bananas and coconuts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Kamehameha II</a> abolished the system in 1819 by sharing a meal of forbidden foods with the women of his court.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hawaiian society rested on a hierarchy of chiefs (aliʻi), priests and commoners (makaʻāinana), bound by the religious restrictions of the <b>kapu</b> system and organised in land divisions, ahupuaʻa, that ran from the uplands to the sea, where irrigated taro, fishponds and fishing fed a dense population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chiefs led in war, settled disputes, organised communal work on fields, terraces, fishponds and mounds, and performed rites that only they could perform, often as descendants of gods; in Hawaii they were protected by <b>kapu</b>, sacred prohibitions whose breach could be punished by death.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>…(aliʻi) traced their descent from the gods and were believed to carry mana, a sacred power greatest in the highest-born, and a web of <b>kapu</b>, sacred restrictions often enforced by death, guarded it: commoners lay face down before the most sacred chiefs, and men and women ate apart.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>The aliʻi nui stood at the head of a hierarchy of chiefs, priests and land managers enforced by the <b>kapu</b> system, and the island's dryland field systems in Kona and Kohala fed a large population.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</a></li><li><blockquote>Chiefs governed through the <b>kapu</b> system of sacred restrictions and a hierarchy of district and land-section chiefs; commoners farmed irrigated taro and tended fishponds.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-kauai">Chiefdom of Kauai</a></li><li><blockquote>Maui's aliʻi nui governed through district chiefs, priests and the <b>kapu</b> system, with irrigated taro in the valleys of west Maui and dryland farming on the slopes of Haleakalā.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-maui">Chiefdom of Maui</a></li></ul></section>
